The recovery sequence was:

Identify duplicate OpenClaw installations.
Remove the obsolete ~/.npm-global copy and ensure /usr/bin/openclaw is the active CLI.
Align the CLI and Gateway versions.
Confirm both execute the same OpenClaw build.

Repair/migrate the configuration.
Back up the configuration, run the appropriate migration/doctor process, and verify with:

openclaw config validate
Identify the incomplete beta.6 release set.
Confirm that core 2026.7.2-beta.6 existed but matching official plugin packages were not published.
Do not blindly follow the moving beta dist-tag.
The core beta tag had already advanced beyond the plugin release line.
Move to the complete 2026.7.2-beta.7 release set.
Explicitly align official plugins.
DeepSeek and WhatsApp upgraded correctly; Brave and llama.cpp required explicit npm installs because 
their recorded ClawHub sources kept them on 2026.7.1.

Verify every plugin at runtime.

openclaw plugins inspect <plugin> --runtime --json
Investigate intermittent Gateway ECONNREFUSED errors rather than assuming authentication failure.

Use systemd evidence to distinguish crashes from intentional restarts.

NRestarts=0
clean SIGTERM
clean shutdown
predictable ~50-second interval

proved the Gateway was being deliberately restarted.

Locate the external restart source.
openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer was executing every 50 seconds.

Identify the stale health-check executable path.

old:
~/.npm-global/bin/openclaw

correct:
/usr/bin/openclaw
Stop the restart loop and harden the watchdog.

Diagnostic failures must no longer mean “restart Gateway.”
Reduce the WhatsApp health-check interval to 15 minutes.
The watchdog now checks four times per hour rather than approximately 72 times per hour.

Perform one final controlled Gateway restart and verify:

openclaw gateway status --deep --require-rpc
openclaw health --verbose

Final result:

OpenClaw 2026.7.2-beta.7 stable
Gateway RPC responsive
Official plugins version-aligned
WhatsApp linked
WhatsApp DM allowlist preserved
WhatsApp groups disabled
No forced 50-second Gateway restart loop

####
Suggested upstream improvements

The OpenClaw upgrade experience could be made more resilient by warning when an older OpenClaw executable 
exists elsewhere in PATH, ensuring beta core releases are not published without matching official plugin 
builds, avoiding recommendations for exact plugin versions that do not exist, and making plugin 
source/version drift more explicit during upgrades.

The local watchdog issue itself was not an OpenClaw core defect, but it illustrates why 
channel-specific monitoring should avoid restarting the entire Gateway on a single failed CLI probe.
